Sorting Methods in DSA

This document summarizes the sorting methods present in the sort folder.

1) Bubble Sort
- Idea: Repeatedly compare adjacent elements and swap them if they are out of order.
- Best for: learning sorting basics and small lists.
- Time Complexity: O(n^2) average/worst, O(n) best for nearly sorted arrays.
- Space Complexity: O(1).

2) Selection Sort
- Idea: Find the smallest value in the unsorted part and place it in the correct position.
- Best for: simple implementation and small arrays.
- Time Complexity: O(n^2) all cases.
- Space Complexity: O(1).

3) Insertion Sort
- Idea: Build the final sorted array one item at a time by inserting each item into the proper position.
- Best for: small arrays or nearly sorted data.
- Time Complexity: O(n) best, O(n^2) average/worst.
- Space Complexity: O(1).

4) Merge Sort
- Idea: Split the array into halves, sort each half, and merge them back together.
- Best for: large datasets and stable sorting.
- Time Complexity: O(n log n) all cases.
- Space Complexity: O(n).

5) Quick Sort
- Idea: Pick a pivot, partition the array, and recursively sort the partitions.
- Best for: fast general-purpose sorting.
- Time Complexity: O(n log n) average, O(n^2) worst case.
- Space Complexity: O(log n) average, O(n) worst.

6) Keden's Sort
- Idea: Included as an additional custom/reference sorting note in the folder.
- Best for: understanding custom implementations and comparison with standard algorithms.
- Note: If you are referring to the file named kenen's.py, it is actually a maximum-subarray program rather than a conventional sorting algorithm.
- Complexity: Depends on the custom logic written in that file.

Note: The file named kenen's.py in this folder is not a standard sorting algorithm; it contains a maximum subarray problem implementation.

Summary Table

Method            Time                Space      Best Use
Bubble Sort       O(n^2)              O(1)       Small/simple examples
Selection Sort    O(n^2)              O(1)       Simple sorting
Insertion Sort    O(n) best / O(n^2)   O(1)       Small/nearly sorted lists
Merge Sort        O(n log n)          O(n)       Large stable datasets
Quick Sort        O(n log n) avg      O(log n)   Fast general sorting
Keden's Sort      Custom/reference    Varies     Included as an extra method


